<h2>The execution that became a trial</h2>
<p>The petitioner&rsquo;s paper position looked complete: purchase documents of December 2015, a tenancy at Rs 15,000 a month, and an ex parte decree for possession and arrears from 2018 (CS 260/2018, decreed 10.12.2018). But when she sought possession in execution, respondents 2 and 3 appeared with a rival story &mdash; that they had bought the property from respondent 1 in 2014, a year before her documents. The executing court dismissed respondent 1&rsquo;s attempt to reopen the decree under Order IX Rule 13, but on the objectors&rsquo; claims it framed issues: had they purchased earlier, and were their documents genuine?</p>

<h2>Why the issues had to be framed</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Rule 97 is a right, not a nuisance.</strong> Any person resisting possession — including a stranger to the decree — is entitled to have the resistance adjudicated. The Supreme Court in <em>Shreenath v. Rajesh</em> (1998) read the scheme purposively: objections are decided inside the execution, not by a fresh suit.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>All questions, including title.</strong> Rules 98 to 101 direct the executing court to determine all questions arising between the parties to the proceeding, including right, title and interest — and bar a separate suit. The determination operates as a decree and carries its own appeal.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Evidence, not affidavits.</strong> Competing purchase claims and genuineness of documents cannot be resolved summarily. Framing issues was not perversity; it was the procedure working as designed.</div>
</div>

<h2>Article 227&rsquo;s narrow gate</h2>
<p>The High Court restated the boundary: supervisory jurisdiction corrects jurisdictional error and perversity; it does not sit in appeal over interlocutory case-management in execution. With the impugned order showing neither defect, interference was refused &mdash; but, conscious of the Supreme Court&rsquo;s insistence in <em>Rahul S. Shah</em> (2021) that executions not outlive the parties, the court asked for disposal preferably within six months.</p>

<h2>Positions after the order</h2>
